Use of Switch: 8/10. Switch does real work: scoped addressing policies refuse the bad sender, only the author can answer approval cards (others get a 403), it reads the event stream, and it posts into Slack channels. The seal and verify core still runs without Switch on an offline stand-in, so Switch carries the trust flow but not the main idea.

Technical execution: 9/10. The evidence is unusually strong: CI on Ubuntu and Windows, a clean clone that reproduces on a second machine, agents killed with SIGKILL and restarted, a forged-proof check, 9 fixtures and log files with message ids. Live setup is heavy, and the two-machine case is not yet checked.

Creativity and impact: 7/10. Letting reviewers ask questions of another team's agent session, with Merkle-proven, author-approved disclosure, is a fresh idea. The problem is real but niche, and the README gives no proof that teams want it.

Presentation and documentation: 6/10. The README is precise and honest about its limits. It is also dense and full of undefined terms (leaves, page rule, G1 to G8, K1 to K4, gateway). No video is visible, and a newcomer would struggle to see the value in 30 seconds.

Shortlist: yes. The engineering and the reproducible evidence put it above most entries, even though its pitch is hard to read.

Weaknesses that cost the most:
1. Jargon and density. The README never shows a short walkthrough of what a reviewer sees and why it beats asking the author in chat, and that costs presentation and impact points.
2. The human loop in Slack is incomplete. Switch reports messages typed in Slack with no user id, so people must answer questions through the gateway or `flip chat`. That weakens the "real team in team chat" story and the Switch-centrality claim.
3. Switch can be replaced. An offline stand-in reproduces the full flow, and the cryptographic core runs without Switch. On top of that, the two-machine run is unverified and only Claude Code sessions are supported, which limits real deployment.

TOTAL: 7.6
